drift file vs served draft, misconception fields: 0 of 26

Total Quality Management
   Students reach for TQM whenever an extract mentions faults, as though it could be switched on this quarter. Its cost arrives first and its return late, which is why it is abandoned more often than disproved. A fault rate that must fall before the next order ships is a different question.

Continuous Improvement (Kaizen)
   Students dismiss kaizen because no single change is worth much, and a repositioned rack reads as trivial beside a new machine. The size of a step is the point of it, not the objection to it: small enough to try is cheap enough to undo. Judge kaizen on what a year of steps adds up to.

